VISUAL DIRECTION SELECTED — phase 2 of 2. Implement the selected design now.

Selected id: direction-b
Title: The Rail — pixel-quantised duotone, horizontal travel
Design image paths (open and inspect these actual files): design/b-1-page.png, design/b-2-transition.png, design/b-3-mobile.png
Plan: THE IDEA
A fashion film, not a catalogue. Near-black #0B0B0A, bone #F3EFE6 and one signal orange #D9531E; every photograph pushed through the same hard duotone so that wildly different source images read as one campaign. Anton at 200px+ against Space Grotesk and mono. The recurring device is block size: images arrive coarsely pixel-quantised and resolve as they reach the centre of attention, so 'coming into focus' is literal and the treatment is the same in the hero, on the rail and in the thumbnails.

PRIMARY JOURNEY (see b-1-page.png)
1. Hero: a shop rail at coarse block size behind 'THIRTY STYLES A YEAR.' 2. The rail: a pinned scene where vertical scroll drives garments travelling sideways past camera on three depth layers at different rates, the centred garment fully resolved and its neighbours still in blocks. 3. Buy panel: the resolved garment fills the frame with its number, name, detail sentence, fabric, colours, size row with the sold-out size struck, price and Add to bag. 4. The index, inverted to bone: the sticky filter with all five states and counts, a 3-column grid carrying every garment's full content and size row. 5. Bag + Measure on black. 6. Keeping it (four care facts). 7. Getting it / What people said on bone. 8. The three shop facts and an oversized wordmark.

MOTION (see b-2-transition.png)
Entry: hero held at coarse block size, slow drift. Development: scroll drives horizontal rail travel; block size is a function of each item's distance from centre, so garments de-pixelate as they arrive and re-pixelate as they leave — the mechanism and the parallax share one scroll value. Exit: the centred garment scales into the buy panel and stays resolved; the block grid persists behind it as texture, so the join reads as arrival rather than a cut. Touch: the rail is also directly draggable with momentum, and drag and scroll write to the same progress value.
Reduced motion: no travel and no scrub — the rail becomes a normal horizontally scrollable strip, images render at their resolved block size, section changes are instant. All buying controls unaffected.

ASSETS
Sourced contextual clothing and workroom photography (rail, hangers, wool coat detail, denim detail, knit, trousers, shorts, folded knitwear), all duotoned to one family. The pixel quantisation is done on canvas at render time, not baked in, so it can be animated.

HONEST WEAKNESS
This is the reason I am not recommending it. Without a callable image generator, these photographs are representative, not the described garments — and at full-bleed hero scale that shows. The Lambswool Crew stand-in is visibly a puff-sleeved mannequin knit; the Oxford Shirt is a rail of hangers. The page says 'Representative imagery — not a photograph of this garment', which is truthful, but it means the biggest, most cinematic images on the site are also the least informative about what you are buying, and the shopper ends up buying from the text. If you supply real garment imagery, or add an image generator to this session, this direction becomes much stronger and I would revisit it.

MOBILE (see b-3-mobile.png)
Hero type drops to 78px over the blocked rail; the rail becomes a swipeable snap strip (thumb-driven, same progress value); index is one column on bone with full-width size buttons; bag is a dark sheet with named lines, Remove, subtotal and the free-delivery bar; Measure inverts back to black.

IMPLEMENTATION
Vite + React. One scroll/drag progress source; the horizontal travel as a transform on a track (native vertical scroll preserved, no smooth-scroll library); pixelation via a small canvas pipeline (draw small, upscale with imageSmoothingEnabled=false) with a resolved <img> swapped in below a block threshold; duotone via blend layers in CSS. Pinned section needs careful interruption handling on iOS.

UNCERTAINTY
Two. The pinned horizontal scene is the classic place where fast/reverse input and mobile browser chrome break the illusion, and per-frame canvas requantisation of several large images is the only genuinely expensive thing in either direction — it would need a resolution ladder and a frame budget.

COST
Higher than A: pinned-scroll mechanics, drag parity, a canvas performance ladder, and a light/dark inversion system across the page.
